From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: a program is told the display changed when the display scale does

After a new display scale (0890) Chrome laid its window out at the new DPI
(WM_DPICHANGED) but drew its page and toolbar at the old one -- cut off at
100%, white where they did not reach at 175% -- until it was reloaded:
Chromium reads the displays' scale again on WM_DISPLAYCHANGE (and a
switch to it), and nothing sent one. On Windows a program's view of the
screen changes with the scale and it hears WM_DISPLAYCHANGE. Now each
process, when it first sees the new scale, posts WM_DISPLAYCHANGE (the
mode's depth and size) to each of its top-level windows, after the
WM_DPICHANGED being handled. Chrome, Edge and Electron programs follow a
change in both directions, crisp.

Test: test/dpifollow-gate.sh part 7 (mutant SG_MUTANT_NO_DISPLAYCHANGE in
sysparams.c); Chrome 154 checked by hand at 2736x1824, 100% -> 175% -> 100%.
---
--- a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
@@ -3968,6 +3968,36 @@ static BOOL get_primary_source_mode( DEVMODEW *mode )
     return ret;
 }
 
+/* Stained Glass OS: the display scale changed (window_check_dpi_change, once
+ * in each process): each of the process's top-level windows is told the
+ * display changed, as on Windows, where a program's view of the screen
+ * changes with the scale. Chromium (Chrome, Edge, Electron programs) reads
+ * the displays' scale again only then: it laid itself out at the new DPI
+ * but drew its page and toolbar at the old one, white where they did not
+ * fit, until something else made it read them. Posted: it comes after the
+ * WM_DPICHANGED being handled now. */
+void post_display_change(void)
+{
+    DEVMODEW mode = {.dmSize = sizeof(DEVMODEW)};
+    DWORD pid, me = GetCurrentProcessId();
+    HWND *list;
+    BOOL ok;
+    int i;
+
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_NO_DISPLAYCHANGE
+    return;
+#endif
+    ok = get_primary_source_mode( &mode );   /* (it takes the display lock itself) */
+    if (!ok || !(list = list_window_children( 0 ))) return;
+    for (i = 0; list[i]; i++)
+    {
+        if (!get_window_thread( list[i], &pid ) || pid != me) continue;
+        NtUserPostMessage( list[i], WM_DISPLAYCHANGE, mode.dmBitsPerPel,
+                           MAKELPARAM( mode.dmPelsWidth, mode.dmPelsHeight ) );
+    }
+    free( list );
+}
+
 static void display_mode_changed( BOOL broadcast )
 {
     DEVMODEW current_mode = {.dmSize = sizeof(DEVMODEW)};
--- a/dlls/win32u/win32u_private.h
+++ b/dlls/win32u/win32u_private.h
@@ -206,6 +206,7 @@ extern void sync_monitor_dpi(void);
 extern UINT set_thread_metrics_dpi( UINT dpi );
 extern void follow_display_scale( UINT dpi );
 extern BOOL system_dpi_follows_scale(void);
+extern void post_display_change(void);
 extern BOOL update_display_cache( BOOL force );
 extern void reset_monitor_update_serial(void);
 extern void user_lock(void);
--- a/dlls/win32u/window.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/window.c
@@ -6688,6 +6688,8 @@ void window_check_dpi_change( HWND hwnd )
         {
             cursors_dpi = new_dpi;
             user_driver->pSystemParametersInfo( SPI_SETCURSORS, new_dpi, NULL, 0 );
+            /* and the process's windows that the display changed (1125) */
+            post_display_change();
         }
     }
     thread_context = set_thread_dpi_awareness_context( context );
